{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1533505-koschei-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf","id":1533505,"title":"Koschei as an enemy faction in Werewolf","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-04-03T00:02:22-07:00","id":1533505,"title":"Koschei as an enemy faction in Werewolf","content":"This is another thread of making the various Talons of the Wyrm as actual factions of the Wyrm that can be introduced into regular chronicle play. I like creating individual factions of the Wyrm with their own lore and antagonists in order to make the setting more interesting than simply making everything part of PENTEX or the Black Spiral Dancers. Diversity of antagonists is good, even if the Garou see them all as minions of the Wyrm. The previous threads can be found here.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1477060-narlthus\">Narlthus</a><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1521708-thrassus-exhibit-338-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf\">Thrassus</a><br /> <br /> And similar threads to beings who are not actual Talons of the Wyrm, but lesser beings.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1504244-the-followers-of-set-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf\">Setites</a><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1514259-the-followers-of-odin-as-a-werewolf-setting-element-foe\">Odin</a><br /> <br /> At first glance, it does not seem Koschei needs much developing. After all, of all the Talons of the Wyrm he has received the most attention being a core element of the sourcebook Rage Across Russia. However, we get fairly limited information about the Talon (although more than any other Talon). Instead, most information is about Baba Yaga with Koschei merely being a plot coupon or macguffin. Furthermore, Koschei is limited to being a figure whose relevance is limited to Russia. If you run a chronicle in North America, western Europe, North Africa, or anywhere else, it doesn't seem like you can utilize the Talon.<br /> <br /> So this thread is about trying to universalize the Talon while preserving his unique lore. To me, the key is to keep Koschei as a kind of dark fairy tale type creature. He is the Koschei of Russian legend. He is also something like Sauron from Tolkien. Or any other &quot;Dark Lord&quot; character like Voldemort among many others. Something more accessible than the more cosmic horror we built around Thrassus. Look for archetypical aspects of the Koschei legends that can be found elsewhere. So what can we do with him? Well, these are my thoughts.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lu7NSJ_t6Yc\" data-code=\"lu7NSJ_t6Yc\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/lu7NSJ_t6Yc/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> <i>&quot;She comes of your father Adam's ... first wife, her they called Lilith. And she was one of the Jinn. That's what she comes from on one side. And on the other she comes of the giants. No, no, there isn't a drop of real Human blood in the Witch.&quot;</i><br /> <br /> - C.S. Lewis, The Lion, the Witch, and the Wardrobe (1950)<br /> <br /> It is known by many names.<br /> <br /> He Who Points.<br /> <br /> Tsar Koschei.<br /> <br /> The General of Winter Unending<br /> <br /> Koschei is \u201cHe Who Points\u201d. It is a primal manifestation of the Wyrm, a Talon of the Wyrm who serves the Eater of Souls. Tsar Koschei is the General of Winter Unending, a malevolent figure of the Apocalypse prophesied to lead the armies of the Wyrm against the forces of Gaia in the end days. &quot;Koschei&quot; is not its real name, but given its presence in the Eurasian forests, it became popularly known by that name, and it remains the name he is best known by, but not the only one. Some Get of Fenris believe the &quot;Loki&quot; of Ragnarok who leads the forces of the world\u2019s destruction is in fact this Talon of the Wyrm. Its true name might not even be known, so to the Garou it is simply &quot;He Who Points&quot;.<br /> <br /> Alone among the Talons of the Wyrm, Koschei chooses to assume a mortal form, that of an old, withered, cruel man. However, it is not human, but an eldritch being of the Wyrm that predates humanity. It either possesses a mortal, transforming the body over time, or uses its own power to create such a form. It often appears to be nothinbg more than a powerful Ferectoi. It assumes this shape in order to infiltrate and corrupt mankind, and the other supernatural beings who have the shape of humanity like fairies and the Changing Breeds. Its true appearance is unknown.<br /> <br /> The Pact of Koschei is comprised of the various vassals of this Talon of the Wyrm. Its vassals are often particularly powerful, supernatural creatures, but also includes humans it has transformed into fomori. It makes pacts with those who are desperate enough for power or aid that they agree to be its loyal, immortal slaves in exchange for power, knowledge, and influence. The vassals of Koschei are some of the most dangerous opponents of the Garou because they recruit and lead many other lesser Wyrm minions in the service of Koschei. Garou legend warns that Koschei will lead a vast host of Wyrm minions against the Garou on the Plain of the Apocalypse in the Near Realm of Battleground.<br /> <br /> More to come.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-03T22:19:08-07:00","id":1533551,"title":"","content":"Although the soul gem of He Who Points is guarded by a small group of Uktena Banetenders in Siberia, and it is best known as Koschei in the lands of Russia, He Who Points is known throughout the world by a variety of local names that have entered human culture and Garou legend. The Talons of the Wyrm are so ancient that all the Garou tribes have myths and legends from those times regardless of the ethnic heritage of their homid breed.<br /> <br /> In western Europe, it has been responsible for the Bluebeard legend. Elsewhere in Asia, He Who Points has been called Div-e Sepid. In the Americas, one name has been Lox. All depict some kind of greedy or lustful monster or sorcerer who imprisons women and difficult to kill permanently. None of these aspects and their followers, however, have been as well known, numerous, or dangerous as those Wyrm minions who follow its aspect of Koschei.<br /> <br /> = = =<br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9Gf7-QtdQss\" data-code=\"9Gf7-QtdQss\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/9Gf7-QtdQss/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> = = =<br /> As a Talon of the Wyrm that arrived on Earth in the earliest days of pre-history, I do not believe Koschei should be limited to Russia. I have no problem with the Talon being most active within a certain geographic area, but even the boundaries of modern Russia does not reflect the boundaries of historic Russia (Kievan Rus) when the legends of Koschei first took form.<br /> <br /> So I think it behooves us to look for similar figures in other cultures. Traditional monsters or sorcerers who have a fondness for mortal women or treasure, and are difficult to kill because they can come back to life.<br /> <br /> Baba Yaga is its most powerful vassal, but other parts of the world should know of the Talon in some capacity and have other vassals of lesser power and influence. That way we can extend the followers of this Talon beyond the confines of Rage Across Russia. That may be its most important battleground, but not the old one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-05T11:24:23-07:00","id":1533624,"title":"","content":"I\u2019ll need to re-read RAGE ACROSS RUSSIA to confirm this, but I don\u2019t believe that Baba Yaga was ever subordinate to the Deathless: however I do like the idea that Koscei is something known far beyond what we would call Russia (After all, if the Uktena on one side of the Bering know about him, it\u2019s likely that their cousins on the other bank had some awareness of the wretch).<br /> <br /> I also find it very easy to imagine this particular entity working to pick up the pieces after the destruction of Baba Yaga: I wonder if Koscei is more or less subtle than the \u2018Little Grandmother\u2019?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-05T13:48:42-07:00","id":1533641,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Atticus Cleanclaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1533505-koschei-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1533624#post1533624\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I\u2019ll need to re-read RAGE ACROSS RUSSIA to confirm this, but I don\u2019t believe that Baba Yaga was ever subordinate to the Deathless:</div> </div> </div> </div>Baba Yaga explicitly owes Koschei a life boon (or some other equivalent pact) because it saved her from destruction by Absimiliard. Of course, the sourcebook also implies that quite possibly Koschei allowed Absimiliard to survive by obtaining a boon from him as well (and that Absimiliard may or may not actually be the Nosferatu antedeluvian because Baba Yaga's 4th generation may be a result of diablerie). Baba Yaga is also working explicitly to find Koschei's soulgem and release him, but the sourcebook quickly subverts this by telling the ST that in no way should Koschei ever be released because it'd change the setting too much.<br /> <br /> Koschei really isn't the villain behind Rage Across Russia though. It's definitely Baba Yaga. Koschei is more like the macguffin that creates the initial motivation and justifies Baba Yaga being in a Werewolf sourcebook instead of staying strictly in Vampire.<br /> <br /> So as written, there's not a whole lot for Koschei (or any of its minions) to do. But since he is the Talon of the Wyrm we know the most facts about, I wanted to expand it into a whole faction. Not anything that would replace Baba Yaga and her army, but try to build on it and allow some measure of having Koschei be an element in other chronicles.<br /> <br /> Also, Baba Yaga definitely has a lot of free agency. It's not like her sole concern is to release Koschei. She's definitely doing other things. But the fact that she made a pact with Koschei that is essentially a life boon means she owes Koschei big time. So that is an aspect of this faction I'll be building on in other posts. Koschei's vassals have a lot of autonomy. They're like feudal vassals who have certain obligations, but otherwise do whatever they want. It's just that when Koschei is finally released at the Apocalypse, he'll be calling in all the cards owed to him because his host will be one of the major armies of Wyrm minions. That's how I'm developing it anyway. I liked the core concept that this Talon of the Wyrm was willing to make pacts with other powerful supernatural individuals, and thought that would be a great hook to build a faction around.<br /> <br /> The relationship between Koschei and Baba Yaga in the legends are nebulous. Sometimes they appear as master and servant, as allies, as antagonists, as rivals, etc. And I think that's great. We can also use that in game so individual STs can do whatever they feel is best for their own chronicles. Vassals aren't necessarily always loyal, and sometimes they become over mighty and rebellious.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-05T23:37:53-07:00","id":1533684,"title":"","content":"He Who Points has seduced and recruited powerful vassals to serve as his dukes and knights all throughout the world. It prefers immortal, or at least long lived, servants for he needs them to be present at the Apocalypse. It has had many vassals who raise armies on his behalf - vampires, mages, fomori, fae, bygones, and other kinds over the many thousands of years since his arrival on Earth. The most important of which was Baba Yaga, the hag of Russia. But they have also included the Giant Who Had No Heart in His Body, the terror of Scandinavia; C\u00fa Ro\u00ed Mac D\u00e1ire, the sorcerer of Ireland and King of Munster; Telpochtli, the Young Man of Mesoamerica; Ahasuerus, the Wandering Jew; and many others. Few have survived to the modern age, but all have been terrible foes of the Garou while in Koschei's service.<br /> <br /> Koschei is the Wyrm's envoy to the Fomorians and resultingly a major menace to the Dreaming. Many chimerical creatures and Bygones, rudely shut out from the mortal world by the Weaver, have flocked to its cause. But its alliances with the dark forces of the Dreaming are not the end of these pacts, just the beginning.<br /> <br /> Koschei's best known pact is with the Hag of Russia, the Nosferatu vampire Baba Yaga. It is even said he may have made a pact with the Nosferatu progenitor himself. As a result many vampires of this lineage have entered its service. Some speculate it is the activities of these vassals who are responsible for the legend of the Nictuku among the Nosferatu.<br /> <br /> Not all vassals of Koschei have supernatural power to begin with. Koschei make these mortals a special kind of fomori, his Sworn. I'll detail these fomori later in another post.<br /> <br /> I've looked for a lot of other cultural examples of monsters/villains who have similar attributes to Koschei. The two things I look for is 1) an immunity to death by placing its soul in an impossible place to find, and 2) an obsession with kidnapping women. I'm often torn whether they should be alternate names of Koschei, or names of vassals under him.<br /> <br /> The legendary figures I think are most similar to Koschei are the stories involving the Norewegian folktake The Giant Who Had No Heart in His Body and Cu Roi Mac Dare.<br /> <br /> This version of the fairy tale has various changes from the actual story, but is close enough to get the point.<br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pokWwET1pCU\" data-code=\"pokWwET1pCU\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/pokWwET1pCU/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> There isn't a cinematic version of Cu Roi Mac Dare, but the essentials of his immortality can be found here.<br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WB-9V0K3ssk\" data-code=\"WB-9V0K3ssk\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/WB-9V0K3ssk/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> In my imagination, He Who Points is a much more human figure than the other Talons of the Wyrm, more accessible. While I see the other Talons as a figure for cosmic horror, I see Koschei as a much more understandable figure. A kind of Sauron lording over the Nazgul. Or the White Witch of Narnia ruling her minions. It would make Koschei an important figure in earlier ages when legendary monsters and such were more present. But a lot of modern day urban fantasy can also find ways to present such a figure appropriately.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-08T04:50:25-07:00","id":1533805,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3046_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3046\" data-vbnamecard=\"3046\">Black Fox</a>, as mentioned above I needed to refresh my memory of The Koschei before offering any real thoughts on this particular entity and, having done so, I freely confess that you were quite right - the Little Grandmother owes The Deathless, though given that what she owes is a fairly miserable existence, it\u2019s interesting to wonder how hard they\u2019ve worked to help each other over the long, long years.<br /> <br /> One thing that did leap out to me is that, when developing The Koschei as an antagonist, it may be useful to come up with stories that give players an idea of how heroes of generations past have battled The Koschei, win or lose: if nothing else the tale of how a villain most famous for battling the Silver Fangs wound up under the watchful eye of Elder Brother would likely be an intriguing one (and these stories would also help give Players an idea of what Ge Who Points is capable of and where his limits are, without giving too much away).<br /> <br /> I wonder when The Koschei was last free to work havoc on the Earth? My gut instinct is to suspect the DARK AGES period, but that covers quite a lot of territory, chronologically-speaking.<br /> <br /> <br /> One last thought: it\u2019s hard not to look at your description of He Who Points and think \u2018Nyarlothotep\u2019.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-08T13:07:15-07:00","id":1533843,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Atticus Cleanclaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1533505-koschei-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1533805#post1533805\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One thing that did leap out to me is that, when developing The Koschei as an antagonist, it may be useful to come up with stories that give players an idea of how heroes of generations past have battled The Koschei, win or lose: if nothing else the tale of how a villain most famous for battling the Silver Fangs wound up under the watchful eye of Elder Brother would likely be an intriguing one (and these stories would also help give Players an idea of what Ge Who Points is capable of and where his limits are, without giving too much away).</div> </div> </div> </div>Like a lot of Garou lore and legends, we don't have a lot to go by. But in my imagination, He Who Points was probably quite active, escaped often, and appeared many times over the ten thousand years or more. So there's probably lots of stories.<br /> <br /> Of course, these appearances of Koschei do not need to be full blown &quot;the Talon has escaped, the Earth is doomed&quot; type crises. I think it might better that while its main power/essence is trapped, that Koschei was able to project some fraction of its power outside the soulgem he was originally imprisoned in. Kind of like how Cthulhu can stir in his dreams and be temporarily active even though the starts aren't quite right yet. That gives us more flexibility while not creating the burden of multiple imprisonments within different gems, each one a new gem that had to be created because the old one was broken. I think it's best to just have one prison - albeit a prison that doesn't seem to be as working as well as it should.<br /> <br /> If I remember correctly, the Uktena guarding the gem don't know what it is they are guarding. They just know its powerful and evil. So they likely just found the fetish prison at some point and correctly determined they needed to guard it. I'll have to reread that part.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Atticus Cleanclaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1533505-koschei-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1533805#post1533805\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I wonder when The Koschei was last free to work havoc on the Earth? My gut instinct is to suspect the DARK AGES period, but that covers quite a lot of territory, chronologically-speaking.</div> </div> </div> </div>In order to do justice to the actual stories about Koschei, I'd probably have his last appearance happen after the Christianization of Kievan Rus (post-982 after the baptism of Vladimir the Great), but before Batu Khan's invasion of 1237/1238. That puts it in the Golden Age type setting these Russian folktales seem to take place in. The Silver Fang twins Yuri and Sophia Tvarivich were supposed to have defeated the Zmei Sharkala the Cruel in 1240. So it makes sense to me that Koschei was last free just before this time when it could be said Silver Fang power was still at its height. I'd probably put it sometime between 1100-1200.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Atticus Cleanclaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1533505-koschei-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1533805#post1533805\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One last thought: it\u2019s hard not to look at your description of He Who Points and think \u2018Nyarlothotep\u2019.</div> </div> </div> </div>Certainly Nyarlathotep has a lot of human guises and interacts with humanity in a way that the other beings of Lovecraft's mythos do not. I did, in fact, read up on some of Nyralathotep's human or semi-human guises in the Call of Cthulhu RPG as I was putting together my notes. However, my main inspiration was to try to do a &quot;Dark Lord&quot; type fairy tale character similar to Sauron or the White Witch that would seem comfortable in human folklore and faerie legend, not a high fantasy setting. Not that those two ideas are incompatible. He Who Points, after all, did not originate from our world.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-08T13:40:10-07:00","id":1533848,"title":"","content":"It\u2019s interesting to note that the original write-up suggests that The Koschei takes a considerable length of time to build up from \u2018recent escapee\u2019 to \u2018literally-Apocalyptic\u2019 (Requiring a significant span of time to reclaim his power).<br /> <br /> This would dovetail neatly with your notion of He Who Points making multiple escapes, but being repeatedly recaptured across the centuries: it seems highly likely that The Koschei would have to engineer exactly the right circumstances to make his escape meaningful or spend every escape playing cat-and-mouse with his gaolers (Forever faced with the competing impulses to do as much damage whilst he can and grow his own power for the sake of making it all the more difficult to re-imprison him).<br /> <br /> This sense of a ticking clock is a key element in any scenario focussing on The Koschei: the longer Our Heroes fail to stop him, the closer the Doomsday Clock edges to Midnight (and I do think making The Koschei a villain who spends years and centuries putting the pieces into place so that their every escape triggers a domino effect adds to the \u2018Sauron\u2019 aspect you\u2019ve suggested for his personality: of all the many, many things that makes Sauron dangerous, his sheer resilience in the face of defeat and disaster impresses me most - when Morgoth was finally beaten, he was DONE, but his apprentice only seemed to grow more dangerous with each defeat).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-08T18:18:54-07:00","id":1533864,"title":"","content":"I think there was something like three years total before a released Koschei would arrive at his full power. Three years would probably be an enormous amount of time in a chronicle (unlike real life, PCs tend to always be doing something and never take any time to relax), but would be extremely short in real life.<br /> <br /> I would have no problem in an end of the world scenario where Koschei only needed three years to gain his full power. But I would want earlier versions of Koschei to require decades or perhaps centuries to increase his powers. But as the passage of time gets nearer to the foretold Apocalypse, that time period shortens. But it's just the nature of time. Something tied to which aspect of the Triatic Wyrm the era is in. Or the result of his original binding weakening.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-08T22:59:00-07:00","id":1533875,"title":"","content":"As an side, I want to note that pointing one's finger can be a threatening or aggressive gesture as it targets or challenges someone. It can also be used to accuse, stigmatize, or blame someone. (Satan, of course, is the great accuser of mankind). Many people associate someone pointing at them to be an attempt to lead or control them them. Its an action people do to animals, or to indicate social authority over someone. It is something associated with leadership and dominating other people, making them subordinates and not equals. Many people find it extremely rude or insulting if someone points at them.<br /> <br /> I will also note that in earlier times, the gesture could be interpreted as the person putting a hex on someone. And since Koschei is seen as a sorcerer in Russian folklore, this might perhaps be most apt.<br /> <br /> But in all of these connotations, Koschei being called &quot;He Who Points&quot; is indicative that the Garou consider him a major threat. It doesn't just need to be a pedantic assignment of a Talon to one of the fingers of the hand. It can be associated with his role as a leader of the Wyrm's forces during the Apocalypse, a corrupter of Gaian forces, and as a warlock that hexes the Garou.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-09T22:40:34-07:00","id":1533936,"title":"","content":"I imagine most vassals of Koschei are existing supernatural creatures seduced by Koschei. But I don't think we should be limited to that. So here are the Sworn of Koschei, who are equivalents of the Nazgul to Sauron. If you can't imagine an existing vampire, corrupt Changing Breed, Mage, wraith, fae, bygone, or some other creature as fitting, you can use this fomori.<br /> <br /> <b>The Sworn of Koschei </b>(fomori)<br /> <br /> The Sworn of Koschei are human fomori selected to be direct vassals of the Talon of the Wyrm. They are all highly capable, ambitious people whose hearts are beholden to one of the Urge Wyrms under the Eater of Souls. They appear human although their minds have become utterly alien. These fomori are not disposable pawns, but important vassals who will lead armies under the banner of Koschei. Koschei only provides his rare fae treasures granting immortality to these servants.<br /> <br /> Some Sworn are created as a result of their lust and greed upon possessing one of the legendary treasures of Koschei. Other Sworn are created as a result of a Seeder bane identifying a human whose heart is already towards the Eater of Souls. There are also humans who transform into Sworn as a result of their own occult investigations into the secrets of the Wyrm. Other vassals of Koschei who see promise in such mortals may also give special knowledge that results in them becoming this type of fomori. The Sworn of Koschei have been mighty bronze age warriors, wise women, tribal chiefs, petty kings, rich merchants, ruthless bankers, ambitious wives, and politicians. All were governed by their greed, ambition, and lust - the vices of the Eater of Souls - all enslaved themselves to Koschei.<br /> <br /> The base powers of the Sworn of Koschei are as follows:<br /> <br /> Powers: Armored Skin, Darksight, Eyes of the Wyrm, Gifted Fomor, Homogeneity, Regeneration, Sense Gaia, Spirit Ties<br /> <br /> Gifts: Persuasion, Master of Fire, Smell of Man, Falling Touch, Bane Protector, Resist Pain<br /> <br /> In addition, each Sworn will have individual custom powers as a result of their unique circumstances or purposes.<br /> <br /> In the Umbra they appear almost invisible but surrounded by shadows.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-11T21:38:07-07:00","id":1534078,"title":"","content":"As a servant of the Eater of Souls, Koschei knows greed, lust, and appetite for power are powerful weapons to corrupt humanity. Koschei had its servants in the Dreaming create magical treasures which it gave to its mortal servants and extend their mortal life like its other servants. Of course the price is them becoming enslaved by its will. In every case Koschei bestowed these treasures as a token of their pact and submission to Koschei after they are transformed into fomori, its Sworn. The treasures of Koschei are creations of the fae that cannot truly be destroyed by mortal means. Any that appear to be broken or destroyed simply appear so as Glamour, and will appear as new again whenever it is safe to do so.<br /> <br /> These treasures can be rings, bracelets, pins, brooches, necklaces, pendants, or any other adornment. All obtained the glory and great wealth they wanted as well as occult powers because of these treasures. All surrendered their will to Koschei as a result. The life extending properties of these treasures only work as a result of one of the Sworn making a pact with Koschei. Otherwise they are inert and powerless. The treasures do not work for any other kind of fomori or other being.<br /> <br /> Koschei does not need to be free or awake to make such pacts. Any human possessing one of these treasures can be put into psychic communication with Koschei. These always first occur in dreams (usually forgotten by the time they wake) when Koschei can interact, evaluate, and tempt the mortal. If he find the mortal unworthy, he will subtly encourage the human to sell, give, or somehow &quot;lose&quot; the treasure so a more worthy mortal may find it. If he finds the mortal worthy, the dreams gradually change as the human begins to embody the vices of the Eater of Souls into their life and grow in power. Only after such corruption has taken hold into their heart does Koschei reveal himself further in dreams and eventually while the person is awake in order to guide them towards becoming fomori and making a pact with him.<br /> <br /> Garou legend holds Koschei has only a finite amount of these fae treasures, and the Garou have documented which of them they have destroyed in Arcadia. But Arcadia has been closed off to the Garou for centuries, and since then none of Koschei's treasures have been destroyed. Instead, they have been hidden in the guarded vaults of the Black Furies, Uktena, and others.<br /> <br /> While each of the treasures of Koscehi extends the life of the Sworn fomori, how it does so is special to each treasure. Some bestow such power naturally, but its possessor ages naturally until they appear as an ancient, withered old man or crone. Some require the Sworn to drink blood or consume human flesh to enable them to regain their youth and vitality. Others are talismans required to perform blasphemous Wyrm rites sacrificing the innocent or relatives to gain their appearance.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-13T23:51:20-07:00","id":1534185,"title":"","content":"Vassals of Koschei typically begin recruiting other, lesser, supernatural creatures to work for them. While many supernatural creatures are already prone to recruit their own kind or already have servants and pawns, the Sworn of Koschei fomori usually lack such ready resources. They must make do with what they can.<br /> <br /> There are many packs of chimerical wolves terrorizing the Dreaming and certain Trods of the Penumbra who owe loyalty to one of Koschei's faerie vassals. Some malevolent Bygones like dragons (such as the Zmei), cockatrices, basilisks, ghuls, manticores, perytons, and harpies who have fallen to the Wyrm also serve vassals of Koschei. Provided they have survived. These likewise either reside in the Dreaming, special realms in the Umbra, under the protection of corrupted Mages, or the Realm itself provided they have access to special powers or pacts that protect them from the forces of Banality and the Weaver.<br /> <br /> Many Dauntain are attracted to the King of Eternal Winter such as the Cursed, Black Magicians, and Apostates. For different reason they view Koschei as one of the supreme forces of banality who will bring about their desires towards the Dreaming.<br /> <br /> Also important are lower fomori fit to be slaves to the Sworn of Koschei. These lesser fomori are known to make allegiances to more powerful and smarter fomori. Newly made Sworn will need time before they can subjugate or attract such lowly minions. One's reputation is not made overnight.<br /> <br /> A very rare number of Banes known as Dream Makers serve Koschei, and a very small number of his servants are aided by one of these Dream Makers. Other Dream Makers continue to serve Koschei, but as independent followers of the Talon rather than as a servant of one of its vassals.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-18T13:42:22-07:00","id":1534412,"title":"","content":"So with a faction like this, everything really depends on how interesting is the individual vassal who commands whatever local Wyrm forces in the chronicle. Simply being a servant of Koschei is not enough.<br /> <br /> Baba Yaga is a great example of such a a vassal. She's well known in folklore. She has an entire sourcebook based around her. She makes a great antagonist. She's the kind of figure you might expect leading her minions onto the Plain of the Apocaylpse on Battleground under the banner of The Talon of the Wyrm.<br /> <br /> Even if other vassals may not be at her stature or power level (I think she should be considered to be the most powerful and influential of Koschei's vassals), they still have to be compelling and interesting to players in that dark fairy tale feel. So now is a good time to give some examples of another vassal of Koschei. I'll start with one and post others later. Interested in seeing if people contribute any of their own ideas.<br /> <br /> <b>Countess of Shrikes</b><br /> <br /> The Countess is a sidhe fae, not a Changeling, who resides in the Far Dreaming able to survive both Banality and Bedlam as a result of her pact with Koschei. She made the Pact during the Shattering in order to preserve herself in its disastrous aftermath. She has attracted many Dauntain to her court, specifically Apostates, Black Magicians, and the Cursed who seek to further the cause of Endless Winter. They believe her survival due to strange magics of banality will allow their people to survive. Many dangerous chimerical creatures serve her. Some members of the Shadow Court exist in a secret conspiracy on behalf of the Countess and her master. Through her Changeling minions, her reach can even extend to the Realm. They try to convert more Changelings to follow the Countess, and sometimes kidnap them to forcibly convert them. Her minions often act like bandits among the Trods. Her followers' conception of He Who Points is of a primal figure, perhaps one of the Fomorians, who is the General or Lord of Winter Unending. Once a feared figure, but whom they have now placed all their hopes.<br /> <br /> The Countess of Shrikes hates mankind for bringing order and reason to the world as it upset the balance and drove the Wyld out of the world, and thus the fae's ability to survive in the Autumn World. She believes the Shattering of the Gates was a defensive move to protect Arcadia, and that the Gates will only open once more with the ending of the world at the Apocalypse. The old world has already been lost, so the only thing to do now is end this world as soon as possible. She delights in sending raiding parties to the Realm that furthers the Apocalypse. By victory over the Garou and those fae or changelings stupid enough to oppose her, she will ensure the survival of her people and their return to Arcadia. A part of her regrets such battles against old allies, but one must do what one must to survive.<br /> <br /> Frequently mistaken as a Lost One, the Countess of Shrikes can in fact leave the Dreaming and is immune to Bedlam. But she prefers to dwell in her home, the Castle of the Impaled Heart located in the Bullydale Wastes of the Far Dreaming, and sends her fae minions and enchanted slaves to do her bidding throughout the Dreaming and the Autumn World. It is said that the Countess of Shrikes can sometimes be found in the Arcadia Gateway trying to convince Queen Marianna to join her. But for now, the Queen sees the Countess as simply a rebellious vassal of hers that will one day be brought to heel, but not right now.<br /> <br /> The machinations of the Countess of Shrikes and her minions are reasons for Changelings to call upon the aid of packs of Stag.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-22T22:36:50-07:00","id":1534574,"title":"","content":"Here is another example vassal of Koschei.<br /> <br /> <b>The Tiger Prince</b><br /> <br /> The Tiger Prince is an ancestor spirit of Bastet Khan from the pre-historic Maru-Diklah War that ravaged the Khan. As part of the losing side, he made a Pact with Koschei as he lay dying. As a result, he survived as a Wyrm corrupted Ancestor spirit that still &quot;guides&quot; and occasionally possesses what descendants he has today. He is one reason why the Khan have destroyed so many of themselves in fraticidal war. He also utterly controls a Den Realm in the Indian jungle which he bestows to a favored Khan descendant. The ancestor spirit manages to rule small band of Khan kinfolk descended from him and those Khan they can produce, typically one every few generations. Since the Khan themselves are almost extinct with around twenty current members, this is not as meaningless as it seems. The Bastet, however, are ignorant as to the actual corrupted nature of this Ancestor spirit when they use his real name. They recognize there is a dangerous spirit known as the Tiger Prince however. The Tiger Prince\u2019s cult most often refers to He Who Points as <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hiranyakashipu\" target=\"_blank\">Hiranyakashipu</a>.<br /> <br /> The influence of the Tiger Prince extends beyond his kinfolk and Bastet descendants. The Ancestor Spirit has gathered a small brood of corrupted spirits (including Jamaks) and Banes around him. Several insane ancestor spirits are part of his brood. A variety of man-eating Changing Breeds provide the spirit known as the Tiger Prince homage in exchange for his favors, protection, and service of his brood. This spirit brood teaches many Gifts to these corrupted Changing Breeds. It is this group of man-eating Changing Breeds that now provide him the most influence since the severe decline of the Khan.<br /> <br /> The Man-Eater camp of Bone Gnawers, some of the Eaters of the Dead of the Silent Striders, some fallen members of the Bringers of Light camp among the Shadow Lords, some corrupted nests of Ratkin, and various individual Garou and Fera who engage in man eating see the Tiger Prince as a powerful spirit ally. There are also some human cannibal occult societies that includes the Tiger Prince as one of their occult benefactors. The Tiger Prince hopes this cult will one day make him an Incarna of man-eating. Some Garou Theurges speculate if this happens it will be one sign of the Apocalypse.<br /> <br /> The portion of the Umbra where the Tiger Prince normally resides is a pocket realm of the Legendary Realm accessible only by the Bastet. There the Tiger Prince relives his revised version of the Maru-Diklah War where he triumphs and mercilessly tortures and destroys his enemies. It is only here that the true identity of the mysterious \u201cTiger Prince\u201d is known by those spirits and rare Bastet who manage to find and enter this pocket realm, at least until they perish.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-24T08:07:28-07:00","id":1534640,"title":"","content":"Just wanted to pop in and reassure you that this is an excellent line of thought: keep well and please keep up the Good Work!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-26T23:22:45-07:00","id":1534798,"title":"","content":"Here is another example of a vassal of Koschei.<br /> <br /> <b>Gello</b><br /> <br /> Gello is one of the surviving Cappadocians of the Cult of Lamia. Raised in the cult as a mortal by her mother priestess and fathered by a nameless slave soon sacrificed after her inception, Gello was embraced by one of the Cappadocian vampires that belonged to the cult. Gello made her pact with Koschei after the diablerie of her clan's Founder and the ensuing pogrom against the Cappadocians by the Giovanni. The Pact preserved her, preventing her discovery despite the many divinatory powers possessed by the Giovanni.<br /> <br /> She now serves Koschei, but reconciles her duty to it and her worship of the Dark Mother by interpreting He Who Points as a servant to the Dark Mother, the Wise Old Man to the Dark Mother\u2019s role as Wise Old Woman. She still controls a mortal group of the Cult of Lamia that has survived uninterrupted since the Neolithic, and even has made several childer in the centuries since her Pact.<br /> <br /> She resides in the wastes of the Atlas Mountains of North Africa, recruiting a host of monsters that haunt the Maghreb and slaughtering the occasional Garou who happens to come across her or her minions. She also has many minions among the local population. Some are ghouls, others mortal members of the Cult of Lamia, and even more are part of a secret society of Berber nationalists unaware they are controlled by an ancient vampire.<br /> <br /> As priestess of the Cult of Lamia, Gello and her small brood has access to many spirits, lore, and magic associated with the Dark Mother. Some mortal members of the cult have similar versions of these powers by using sorcerer Paths. Gello also has connections with various Bahari cults of vampires around the world who know her by reputation even if they do not know her actual clan. She is not a member of the True Black Hand, but those sect members who follow the Path of Lilith observe her with interest.<br /> <br /> The Silent Striders have identified a large area around the southern Atlas Mountains and northern Sahara as a kind of danger or forbidden zone. Since the fall of the dictator Moamar Gaddhafi, she has gained much influence in Libya as a result of its political chaos. Too many Silent Striders have disappeared in it. What few bodies have been found are mocked by strange black pox. They have heard about a mysterious figure called al-Kahina (the soothsayer) rumored to have supernatural powers, but so far have learned little.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-30T22:30:14-07:00","id":1534983,"title":"","content":"I conceive He Who Points as a universal figure, not limited to Russian folklore or geography (although I have no problem making him more dominant or prominent within that geography). So I think he should be present in the New World. This is my attempt at such. I really like the character of Chester R. Van Gelding in Rage Across New York, so this is my attempt at doing that kind of character with Koschei. But I also thought of doing such a character to an actual member of the &quot;Five Civilized Tribes&quot; who is a sworn of Koschei. Who knows, I may still do so.<br /> <br /> <b>Hiram Van Tassel</b><br /> <br /> A Sworn of Koschei, Hiram was a Dutch fur trader in New Netherlands who made his pact with He Who Points (under the name <a href=\"https://www.native-languages.org/loks.htm\" target=\"_blank\">Lox</a>) during the Beaver Wars when he seized Indian jewelry from a dead Mahican. The jewelry was in fact one of the treasures of Koschei. He built a fur trading empire for over a century before expanding into real estate, textiles, and transportation as the newly created United States industrialized. The Van Tassel family's wealth would become the stuff of legend alongside the fortunes of the Astor, Vanderbilt, and Van Gelding families of the early United States.<br /> <br /> Hiram still secretly controls much of the Van Tassel fortune, but poses as a distant member of the Van Tassel family who always manages to be a member of the board of the Van Tassel Family Trust. As a result of his contacts from the fur trade, Hiram made alliances with a variety of bizarre Wyrm menaces throughout North America. Various Adlets, Basket Women, Kushtaka, Skin-walkers, Stick Indians, Water Babies and Wechuge serve Hiram as the emissary of He Who Points. Hiram's mortal family extends beyond those of the official Van Tassel heirs, as he had many wives among the Indian allies he traded furs with and sired many children.<br /> <br /> Hiram controls the Van Tassel Museum (officially the Van Tassel Museum of Colonial America and Native American Heritage) in New York City. Previously just the Van Tassel Museum of Colonial American History, it changed its name in the 1990s as a move towards political correctness. What has not changed is Hiram using the museum to hunt for additional treasures of Koschei that were possessed by the native Americans in the guise of historical preservation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-30T22:33:29-07:00","id":1534984,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Atticus Cleanclaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1533505-koschei-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1534640#post1534640\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just wanted to pop in and reassure you that this is an excellent line of thought: keep well and please keep up the Good Work!</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks. It's actually very supporting since this thread has provoked a lot less responses than my other threads on brainstorming about various factions of the Wyrm.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-04T20:13:43-07:00","id":1535155,"title":"","content":"One reason I am posting various vassals of Koschei is that I want to make He Who Points a more universal figure so an ST can justify referring to the Talon regardless of the geography or time of her chronicle. Too many iconic concepts of the game seem to be limited to a specific time and place, so an ST who wants to be creative and set her chronicle in a non-standard geography or history could be left without many antagonists to be used. But I am trying to keep the &quot;dark fairy tale&quot; feel that seems associated with He Who Points for all of these vassals.<br /> <br /> <b>Nadilla</b><br /> <br /> Nadilla is a Bygone, a Ghul, who resides in Iraq. She has hunted the wastes of Mesopotamia for thousands of years, but made her pact during the time of the Assyrian Empire in order to escape a potent vampire whose territory she had poached. While ghuls are naturally long lived, her pact with \u201cDiv-e Sepid\u201d enabled her to survive far longer than usually in a hostile land. She has outlived the empires of the Achaemenids, Alexander, Rome, the Abassids, Seljuks, and the Ottomans.<br /> <br /> Her last previous guise was as a wife to one of Saddam Hussein's sadistic generals before reinventing herself by devouring a returning Iraqi exile who worked as a human rights activist. This shapechanging cannibal is a cunning actress who always makes sure she has easy access to her next corpse meal. She is a sometimes ally to the Tal'Mahe'Ra and has ensorcelled several Nagaraja vampires to her will whose diet is similar to her own. She knows the resting spots of several ancient, torpored vampires. She protects them in exchange for favors of these Methusalehs' servants. Packs of fomori jackals and hyenas obey her call whenever she needs them. She's also made pacts with a local Nephwrack cult who dwell in a Haunt she occupies and can summon various Spectres to perform Oblivion's work in the Skinlands. Other less powerful Ghuls serve her in exchange for her favors and protection. While the fertile farms and cities of Mesopotamia are mostly safe from her grasp, she rules the desert wastelands.<br /> <br /> Nadilla has accumulated a large fortune over the millennia she has survived, and buried great caches of wealth around the land of the Two Rivers. She is one of the most cunning creatures alive and has often survived shapechanged into animal form. However, she prefers the aspect of a beautiful, seductive woman, especially someone she's recently devoured. Her hobbies are to employ sadism and cruelty to ruin human lives, but has gathered a respectable force of supernatural protectors to fend off any who might seek to harm her.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-06T12:40:36-07:00","id":1535201,"title":"","content":"I've been keeping an eye on this and it's made me go back to Hellboy. While that Koshchei does not bring much useful here, the visuals are definitely striking.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-06T23:18:42-07:00","id":1535211,"title":"","content":"Despite being a big Hellboy fan, I've been very inconsistent in the last decade at picking up the Hellboy/BPRD comics. I just don't go to comic book stores regularly anymore. So I'm not up to date on its take with Koschei. But I loved its take on Baba Yaga and Hecate. However, Mignola and his sub-authors (Arcudi, Allie) do a great job at integrating the different genres of horror (traditional, folk, cinematic, cosmic, etc.) which is perfect for my own take on how the Wyrm should be presented in Werewolf.<br /> <br /> Of course, I think WoD's take on &quot;Koschei&quot; is just to use the figure as a skin for its Talons of the Wyrms which is very different than the folklore Koschei. Hellboy is likely taking a more &quot;loyal&quot; take on Koschei. However, I still want to retain that fairy tale feel. It's likely Hellboy's Koschei would be more like a vassal of He Who Points rather than He Who Points itself.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-08T11:11:36-07:00","id":1535286,"title":"","content":"It's an older story about Baba Yaga and Koschei. It's Darkness Calls from 2007. Koschei is an intractable slave to Baba Yaga who cannot die until the witch releases him. He is an intractable foe that seeks to eliminate Hellboy while respecting him and feeling that death is the release and that he can end both his own and Hellboy's suffering if Hellboy would just die.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-13T14:44:11-07:00","id":1535531,"title":"","content":"Going back to Baba Yaga herself in Rage Across Russia, does anyone still use her in a Werewolf chronicle? If so, is it just a running Rage Across Russia chronicle, or is she still some general setting detail in chronicles that do not take place there?<br /> <br /> If we go with the idea that Baba Yaga was destroyed (whether by the Garou, or as in Nights of Prophecy), what happened to her armies and her quest to recover Koschei's soul egg? Did they just fall apart without her?<br /> <br /> What would her new child Segei Voshkov do? He was extraordinarily powerful for being a vampire of just a few years old, but I think that was typical early edition sleight of hand that said young vampires could be given lots of powerful Disciplines temporarily by more potent, older vampires (like how Maldavis had many powers when the Primogen first backed her against Lodin, but they disappeared after they ended their support). Which would mean Voshkov would now have a more reasonable power spread and just have potent blood. Left by himself would he abandon Baba Yaga's plan and go back to doing whatever he wants on his own, or could he somehow be tempted to join Koschei by some means and begin rebuilding her forces?<br /> <br /> Could one of the Zmei take over? They'd be more likely to ally with Koschei on their own, and one does have the ability to shapechange into human form (Shazear) but certainly Rustarin with her Commanding Gaze power could become a leader, perhaps using Shazear as her tool to control mortals. Thought it is debateable whether the Zmei would elect to serve Koschei on their own.<br /> <br /> Another possibility is one of the Mages in her Army of the Arcane, who transfer their loyalty from Baba Yaga to Koschei thinking he can somehow help them inject more Dynamism into static reality. Like the Zmei, they were also tasked to find Koschei so they could know enough to try to make some kind of deal with it even if they never find the soul egg itself.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-27T07:51:19-07:00","id":1536359,"title":"","content":"I\u2019d imagine that, with the demise of Baba Yaga her forces would fragment (With opportunists running for cover and the True Believers doing their best to salvage something from the wreck).<br /> <br /> In fact you could very easily make power struggles after the Final Death of the \u2018Little Grandmother\u2019 into an occult parallel to the confusion following the meltdown of the Soviet Union (Though hopefully the PCs will be able to ensure a happier outcome).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-27T16:44:52-07:00","id":1536392,"title":"","content":"If we're going to say Baba Yaga is gone and go down the list of her servants, this is what I'd do with each one.<br /> <br /> Viktor - 2000 year old Gangrel who was blood bond to Baba Yaga and is &quot;General of the Night&quot;, leading nearly 25 vampires to carry out political and urban operations. It's said that only his love for Baba Yaga (which I imagine is something like a grandchild's love for a beloved grandmother) has kept him from &quot;going into the earth to rest.&quot; So with her death I imagine he goes into prolonged torpor.<br /> <br /> Snaps-at-Shadows - Black Spiral Dancer who is the general of the Army of War, the collection of Black Spiral Dancers, corrupted Garou, and fomori who attack caerns and drain them. Probably just becomes an &quot;ordinary&quot; Black Spiral Dancer afterwards since not much has changed, but possibly lost influence among the tribe now that she's no longer supported by Baba Yaga. Possibly killed by the Garou during the past few decades.<br /> <br /> The Zmei - Can they still exists in the Realm now that Baba Yaga is destroyed? They only managed to remain within the realm as a result of her original summoning and binding. I think a strong case can be made that they should disappear back in the Umbra. They're probably among the most gonzo and overpowered elements in Rage Across Russia so many STs might want them gone. Might possibly let Shazear be the last Zmei to still be around if the ST wanted one to remain because he often shapechanges into human and has been active for so long. He's the least disruptive of the Zmei.<br /> <br /> Joseph Stranzvitch - Celestial Chorus leader of the Army of the Arcane. Assuming he became so corrupted by Baba Yaga that he is probably considered to be barabbi. Their major duty was to uphold and strengthen the Shadow Curtain, but also assisted the Army of Despair in trying to find Koschei. I assume with Baba Yaga's death, they have gone their own way. But Joseph (and his coven) remains a candidate to try to serve Koschei.<br /> <br /> Tamara Andreeva and the Volinchaya coven of Verbena - Since they were set up to be redeemable good guys to help the Garou in RAR, I assume they've recovered and have nothing to do with Koschei or Baba Yaga's remaining forces.<br /> <br /> Bezariel - the demon leader of the Army of the Void. Probably banished back into the Umbra.<br /> <br /> Sergei Voshkov - childe of Baba Yaga. A former spy and master manipulator, he's likely lost much of the powers he was given in RAR. But his potent blood and natural talents would still make him a powerful vampire for his age. The danger is that he's diablerie bait. Still retains control of most of his spy network, the &quot;Eyes of Baba Yaga&quot;. Probably is part of the Camarilla using an assumed name, hiding his true identity. Still working on restoring Russia to great power status. He'll either stay behind scenes forever or eventually becomes a future Prince of Moscow, Justicar, or sit on the Inner Circle depending on the time frame.<br /> <br /> So if I wanted to remove Baba Yaga from the setting, but retain some sort of faction still loyal to Koschei using existing characters, I'd most likely have Joseph Stranzvitch be its new leader. Probably just call him the Black Monk or the Mad Monk to hide his identity and have yet another &quot;Rasputin&quot; analogue in the setting who can recruit new followers to help him serve Koschei whom he imagines is some kind of fairy tale king of Old Russia around which he can restore an Old Believer type church and turn back the clock to a more pure &quot;Russia&quot;. Unlike Sergei Voshkov who follows the traditional Russian state, I imagine Stranzvitch to have a less state oriented model and more about some sort of cultural Old Russia that no state today could possibly embody. Very mystical and esoteric oriented.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}